WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
Most people are letting AI destroy their

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.600
ability to think, training AI to become

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.360
their own replacement. Tragic, because

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.160
AI can make you dangerously intelligent.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.040
I went from being homeless to an MIT

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.240
grad and running and advising AI

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.000
companies worth billions. And here's

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:22.280
what I've learned. The top 1% use AI

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.240
backwards. They don't prompt to get

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.560
answers. They use it to train their

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.760
brain and outsmart [music] almost any

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.280
situation. So in this video, I'll break

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:35.120
down a counter intuitive system the top

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.520
1% use to get smarter faster with AI.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:41.080
Here is the four-step framework. Step

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.000
one, intelligent laziness. A study in

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.520
Harvard Business Review found that CEOs

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.815
waste 72% of their time in meetings that

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:47.880
don't move the needle.

00:00:47.815 --> 00:00:49.800
>> [music]

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.920
>> We've all experienced those meetings,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.360
haven't we?

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.720
The one-hour meeting that needed only 15

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.160
minutes to get to a decision, but it's

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.760
hard to stop. So why do some of the most

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.800
accomplished folks feel trapped [music]

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.280
this way? Because we all suffer from

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.320
this biological glitch called completion

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:12.200
bias. Your brain is wired to seek an

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.800
immediate dopamine hit that you get from

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.080
finishing a task. So we end up treating

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.080
all tasks as equal because we're going

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.160
to get roughly the same amount of

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.800
dopamine when you spend time on

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.640
redrafting an internal email or a

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.720
million-dollar strategy document.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:32.280
Everything is priority one. So none of

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.400
it is. So how do you avoid this priority

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.360
blindness? A good way to think about

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.960
tasks is to see two curves. [music]

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.600
First curve has capped payoffs. This

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.880
curve goes up and then flattens out once

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.880
it reaches the zone of diminishing

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.920
returns. So tasks like formatting slides

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.120
or internal [music]

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.320
emails, expense reports, FYI meetings.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.280
What happens if you spend additional

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.480
effort to make the outcome of these

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:03.796
tasks pitch perfect? Nothing. There's no

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.320
upside here because the value flatlines

00:02:03.796 --> 00:02:07.360
[music]

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.320
after a point. Nobody cares if you spend

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.000
hours choosing better fonts or

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.960
breathtaking designs in internal slides

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.640
that are seen for 6 minutes. This curve

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.920
shows you your zone of intelligent

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.000
laziness. There was a Nobel Prize

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.440
winning economist and computer scientist

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.320
and his name was Herbert Simon and he

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.400
came up with a concept called

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.880
satisficing, which pretty much means

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.840
stop when it's good enough. Satisfy

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.480
and suffice. [music]

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.640
Satisfice. Now our second curve is the

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.440
exact opposite. It has uncapped payoff.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:45.400
This curve stays flat for a long time,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.840
but then goes to the moon in a hurry.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.840
These are tasks like customer

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.920
interactions, product design, pricing

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.200
model, finding a co-founder or a life

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.720
partner. Being 1% better here does not

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.760
yield 1% better result. It actually

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:05.240
solves the rest of the 99% of your

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:08.320
problems. Pour your soul into this. Jony

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:11.160
Ive would obsess for many months on even

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:14.160
the internal component design of iPhone.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:17.240
But you know, Steve Jobs never said,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.880
"Hey, this is costing us a lot of money.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.400
And who's going to pry open the iPhone?"

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.720
But Steve knew this was the second

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.640
curve. [music] So if the first curve is

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.200
your zone of laziness, your second curve

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:33.640
is your zone of obsession. Let's talk

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:36.560
about how AI can help. The top 1% use AI

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:40.240
on zone one or the zone of laziness. The

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.840
more they outsource zone one to AI, the

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.920
more they can focus [music] on zone two,

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.760
the zone of obsession. So how do I

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.720
decide what to outsource to AI and when?

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.000
So for that, I use a very simple

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:55.920
framework called DRAG framework, D R A

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.840
G. Four categories of work you [music]

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.360
immediately should delegate to AI so you

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:04.480
can stay in your zone of obsession.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:08.120
First, D equals [music] drafting. This

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:10.840
is the blank page problem we all face.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:13.040
It's hardest to get [music] from zero to

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.600
one sometimes. AI can help here

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.040
tremendously, actually. [music] Give it

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:20.037
a prompt using the AIM protocol that

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.800
I've shared before. Hey AI, act in

00:04:20.037 --> 00:04:25.880
[music] this role, use this input, and

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.480
this is your mission. AIM. In that way,

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:31.800
you get started very quickly on that

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.720
email or code or presentation. And the

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:37.840
first draft from AI will be crappy and

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.360
atrocious, but that's fine. Now [music]

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.760
you have a starting point. You're not

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:44.920
staring at a blank page anymore. Now

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.880
it'll trigger something in your brain

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:50.520
and you're off to the races. R equals

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.440
research. This helps you solve the

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.408
information overload problem. Today, if

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.920
something requires deep research,

00:04:55.408 --> 00:05:00.000
[music] it can be dramatically

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:02.760
accelerated using AI. Summarization,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.720
extraction, competitive intel. You know,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:06.720
don't spend time doing that kind of

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:09.360
research. Let your friendly neighborhood

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.760
AI do it for [music] you. When you use

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.760
the deep research feature on ChatGPT or

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:18.360
Gemini or Claude, it fires off hundreds

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.640
of secondary search queries. It goes out

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.880
to the web like a spider and finds

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.320
hundreds of sites, consolidates the

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.848
results, even checks its own work by

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.080
asking what's missing,

00:05:26.848 --> 00:05:30.120
>> [music]

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.320
>> and follows up on its own to finally

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.520
deliver a rich document to you. It's

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.080
like you just hired a consultant for a

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:40.440
week-long research project, but instead,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.680
you get there in 10 minutes. Third is A

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:46.760
for analysis. Let AI take the first pass

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:50.240
at analyzing, summarizing, reasoning,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:53.200
especially if it's all unstructured data

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.880
because AI is going to find patterns

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.080
that we humans aren't going to be able

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.880
to. So use it for your advantage. And

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:03.280
finally, G is for all the grunt work.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:06.240
Tasks like reformatting, translating,

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:10.040
tabulating, cleaning data, and on and

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:12.000
on. The boring manual work. Just give it

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:15.680
to AI. So what's the key principle

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.880
behind DRAG? Apply it only when you are

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:21.080
in your zone one, that first curve. If

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.440
it requires human interaction or

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:28.000
judgment or intuition or decision-making

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.960
or taste, that's curve two. That you've

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.440
got to do it yourself. But you know, I

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:36.320
have found that 70 or 80% of my

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.240
repetitive tasks tend to be in zone one

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:42.200
and you might find that too. So be lazy

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:44.680
when you can use DRAG. Be obsessed for

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:48.240
everything else. Step two, the

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:51.440
intelligent hill. For 300 years, Isaac

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:54.080
Newton convinced us that universe was a

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.040
clockwork machine, predictable

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.960
and certain. But in 1927, another

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:00.160
scientist [music]

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.360
named Heisenberg shattered those

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:06.160
classical beliefs. He showed that our

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:09.080
universe exists only as a cloud of

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.400
possibilities at quantum level. It was a

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:14.040
profound shift. [music] You and I have

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:16.760
to make a similar shift when we use AI

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:19.280
nowadays. The first trick is to stop

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:21.800
treating AI like a [music] calculator.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:25.120
We like to live in a world with clear

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:27.440
rules. You type 2 + 2 into a calculator

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:30.480
and you get [music] four. Always. It's

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.080
predictable. But AI is not a calculator.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.160
It's [music] a probability engine. If

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.400
you ask the same question to AI again,

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:37.360
it'll give you a completely different

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:40.080
answer. It'll happily make things up for

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.280
you unless you ask it to verify. AI is

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.120
brilliant on some days, confused on

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:47.000
others, but on any given day, it refuses

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.600
to admit that it doesn't know the

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:52.560
answer. It loves to make things up. So

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.240
you don't just ask AI the way you ask a

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.600
normal human being. You have to

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.800
architect your questions very carefully.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.200
Now most people use a tactic called

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:03.760
zero-shot prompting. So for example,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:06.600
they would ask, "Give me the best new

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.640
business idea." And of course, AI will

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:10.880
dish out a response and tell you why

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:13.320
it's the greatest idea in the world, but

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:15.440
you're literally rolling the dice and

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:18.360
looking to win. To get elite results

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:21.240
though, you must climb the intelligent

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:24.080
hill. There are four camps on the way.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.720
Each camp will show you a different way

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:29.280
to work with AI. Our first camp is

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.800
called one-shot prompting. When you

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.840
prompt, give one [music]

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.159
clear example so the model doesn't guess

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:38.800
blindly. So the prompt would look like,

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:42.039
"Write a LinkedIn post about remote

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.695
work. Use this specific post as a style

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:42.760
guide."

00:08:42.695 --> 00:08:44.360
>> [music]

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:47.320
>> And so give it a post, give it an

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:50.120
example, and paste that post in the

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:53.280
prompt as a reference. And that simple

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:56.360
act is already an upgrade than rolling

00:08:53.280 --> 00:09:00.240
the dice blindly. Second camp, few-shot

00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:03.280
prompting. Now here you give AI three or

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.480
more examples so it can find patterns of

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:10.360
style and substance and tone that you

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:13.160
desire. Attach documents, links, data,

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:15.760
or your prior work. This is called

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:18.680
grounding the model. So basically it

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:21.480
stops fantasizing and hallucinating and

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:24.200
gets grounded to reality. Here's an

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:27.000
example of a prompt. Here are the five

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.480
of my previous presentations and now

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:33.120
write a new presentation based on my

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:36.200
tone of voice on topic XYZ. And here's a

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:38.920
pro tip. Ask the AI to explain the

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.520
pattern back to you first. That way AI

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:43.240
is forced to articulate what it's doing

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.640
and more importantly, you're forced to

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.800
learn how your brain works. How did it

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:50.080
come up with those patterns? [music] Now

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:52.040
you're being smart about being smart.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.080
Now let's move to the third camp. This

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:56.640
one is called chain of thought

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.400
reasoning. Again, fancy name, but the

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.760
idea is simple. Ask the model to think

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:06.480
long and hard before it responds. Your

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:08.960
job is to slow AI down. Enforce explicit

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.720
clarity by asking it to show its work.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:12.920
That's all there is. This is also a good

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.800
way to reduce hallucinations, of course.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:18.000
So, let's say you're working on some

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:20.120
report, and so you attach it and write a

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.080
prompt that could look like this. Do not

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:25.080
refine my research report yet. List the

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.280
top three most impactful areas of

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:29.400
improvement after we analyze it. Tell me

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.520
why you think so and suggest how we

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:34.960
address each. Think step-by-step. Show

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:37.040
me your thinking for each step. That

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:40.160
last line is the most important one. And

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:43.000
our fourth and final camp is agents.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:46.920
According to Salesforce, AI agents help

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:48.360
drive $67 billion in global sales during

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.320
Cyber Week alone. [music]

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:52.040
So,

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:54.640
agents are already here. The best way to

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.040
think about agents is to think about who

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:59.400
you would hire for a task. So, let's say

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:00.247
if you wanted to hire a researcher, an

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:00.360
analyst,

00:11:00.247 --> 00:11:02.960
>> [music]

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:05.680
>> and a copywriter. You can do that with a

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:08.720
single agentic prompt that looks like

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:12.440
this. Do deep research on trends on

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:14.440
topic XYZ, analyze and cross-reference

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:17.440
all the trends to find the three most

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:20.400
important ones, and draft a one-page

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:22.680
memo summarizing the findings. Now, what

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.600
is actionable? Try this framework

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:28.280
tonight. Open your favorite AI app and

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.880
take any prompt that you were about to

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.600
use. Just try to get to the next camp.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.000
That's how you start climbing up the

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:38.360
intelligent hill. Remember, when you are

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:41.200
dealing with a drunk genius, make sure

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:43.920
you're the one driving the car. So, now,

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:46.440
at this point, everything we've done has

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:48.560
made you fast and efficient. You're

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:51.520
delegating better, you're prompting

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.720
smarter, you're moving up the hill, and

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:56.160
there's less friction than before. And

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:58.800
that's exactly where most people would

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:02.600
stop. But, here's a plot twist. The top

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:05.000
1% go one step further. They slow things

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:07.600
down deliberately. Why is that

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.760
important? The trick that top 1% know is

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:12.400
this. They know when to shift the gear

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:14.360
because long-term intelligence isn't

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:17.120
built through convenience, it's built

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:18.848
through resistance. And that's why we

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.080
need to go to step three,

00:12:18.848 --> 00:12:23.240
>> [music]

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:25.480
>> the intelligent gym. Most people use AI

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:27.680
as wheelchair for the mind. And if you

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:30.400
sit in a wheelchair when you can still

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:31.480
walk, eventually, your legs stop

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.440
working.

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:36.040
Atrophy. And today, it's happening

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:38.960
faster than at any point in human

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:41.000
history. But, the top 1% use a very

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:44.120
different principle. For information

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:47.240
tasks, use AI to remove friction. For

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:49.633
transformation tasks, use AI to add

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:51.720
friction. When you go to a physical gym,

00:12:49.633 --> 00:12:54.080
[music] we all know how muscles are

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:57.040
built, right? Through resistance. You

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.760
lift increasingly heavier weights to

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:01.800
introduce wear and tear to your muscle

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:04.160
fibers, so they break and they grow back

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:06.080
stronger. That is called progressive

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:09.960
overload. But, when it comes to our

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:12.920
minds, we do the exact opposite somehow.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:14.800
We avoid resistance. We use AI to

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:17.600
outsource our thinking. Write my

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.160
LinkedIn post, fix my resume, summarize

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.920
this book. That's like going to the gym

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:25.040
and asking someone else to lift weights

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:28.560
on your behalf. You know, when

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:31.160
astronauts spend months in zero gravity,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:35.680
their muscles and bones atrophy

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:39.040
dramatically, up to 20%. AI is like zero

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:42.440
gravity for your thinking. No friction,

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:44.720
no load, no growth. The intelligent gym

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:47.080
is not about information. It's about

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:49.440
transformation. For things where you

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:53.000
need to be smart and capable, you can

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:54.880
think of AI as your spotter. In any gym,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.680
a spotter doesn't lift the weight for

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:57.560
you.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:00.120
They stand next to you and help you

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:02.200
lift. They also make sure that you don't

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:05.680
get crushed when you're lifting the

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:09.560
weight. So, do the same with AI. Here's

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:13.160
a concrete example. If you want to learn

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:16.120
a concept, study it first yourself, and

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:18.920
then go to your spotter, your AI. Paste

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:21.400
the concept text, and then prompt AI. I

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:23.480
need to master this concept. Quiz me on

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:26.600
it. And now comes the most important

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:29.400
part of your intelligent gym. Ask AI to

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:31.600
apply progressive overload. Four levels.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:34.200
Level one, quiz me like I am a high

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.160
school student. Level two,

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:38.000
ask me questions like I am a college

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:40.560
student. Level three,

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:42.880
now grill me like you're interviewing me

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:45.480
for an executive job. And level four,

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.800
now challenge me like an irate boss who

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:49.200
thinks I'm unprepared. So, that truly

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:51.760
strengthens and deepens your

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:54.200
understanding on that concept. So, now

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:56.720
we have covered three key steps to learn

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:59.040
how the top 1% become smarter [music]

00:14:56.720 --> 00:15:01.360
by using AI. But, there is one internal

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.079
adjustment that changes everything, and

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:07.680
that is our final step. Step number

00:15:04.079 --> 00:15:10.600
four, the intelligent fool. You know,

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:13.600
the biggest obstacle to intelligence

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:16.720
isn't ignorance, it's ego. That's why

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:18.920
the smartest people are obsessed with

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:21.440
what they don't know. And this is what I

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:23.800
call the fool's advantage. [music] Let

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:25.880
me give you an example. Microsoft went

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:28.959
from $300 billion

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:32.079
to $300 trillion in market cap [music]

00:15:28.959 --> 00:15:34.520
with just one mental cultural shift.

00:15:32.079 --> 00:15:37.320
When Satya Nadella became the CEO of

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:40.480
Microsoft in [music] 2014, they had

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:43.160
missed two huge disruptions, search and

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:45.120
mobile. The cloud race was ongoing, but

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:47.140
it was slipping away from them with

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.760
Amazon becoming the 800-lb gorilla,

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:50.320
[music]

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:53.680
and the culture inside the company was

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:55.655
toxic and political, and everyone was

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:58.280
terrified to admit that there were gaps

00:15:55.655 --> 00:16:00.600
[music] in their knowledge. Satya made

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:03.320
one cultural move. He told the entire

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:05.120
company, "We're switching from a culture

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:08.160
of know-it-alls

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:10.320
to learn-it-alls." A complete reboot of

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:12.440
Microsoft culture. The smartest people

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:14.160
in the room were finally given

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:16.000
permission to say,

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:18.959
"I don't know." Or, "I was [music]

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:22.600
wrong." And to embrace that beginner's

00:16:18.959 --> 00:16:24.760
mind. Now, Wall Street was skeptical at

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.441
first, but the market cap eventually

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:26.800
went from $300 billion

00:16:26.441 --> 00:16:28.760
>> [music]

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:31.720
>> to over $3 trillion. And it keeps

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:33.400
growing. 10x growth in a decade. And

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:35.880
here's why this matters. [music]

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:38.440
Neuroscience tells us that our brain can

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:41.120
rewire all the time. It's called

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:44.000
neuroplasticity. This rewiring happens

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:46.720
only at the edge of your ability. It

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:48.440
happens when you are making errors. It

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:50.800
happens [music] when you're frustrated,

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:53.480
when you're feeling that discomfort. And

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:56.000
if you aren't feeling stupid, you aren't

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:58.160
learning. And aren't you glad that AI

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:00.400
has just handed you the ultimate

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:03.040
training ground to be a student again?

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:05.400
You can bring your beginner's mind to AI

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:07.480
all day long. Ask questions you would

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:10.040
never ask your colleagues out of fear of

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:12.120
embarrassment. AI doesn't roll its eyes.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:14.320
Pick one thing that you don't understand

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:16.800
in your field, something that everyone

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:19.760
else thinks you know, but you know you

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:21.320
don't. And then ask AI the most basic

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:23.439
questions about that topic [music] that

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:26.120
you can think of. And then ask, "Can you

00:17:23.439 --> 00:17:28.880
explain it to me in a simpler way? Teach

00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:30.920
me like I am 10 years old." I ask these

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:33.080
questions all the time. In fact, I ask

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:35.560
three times in a row to simplify again

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:38.480
and again. And sure, I guarantee you,

00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:40.400
you'll feel ridiculous at first. I do

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.160
all the time. But, that's the whole

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:45.960
point. Have the courage to play the fool

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:48.400
today so you can be the genius tomorrow.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:49.679
The trick to mastery is going back to

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.600
simplicity itself. [music]

00:17:49.679 --> 00:17:54.160
If you examine some of the greatest

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:57.440
masters across human history, you'll see

00:17:54.160 --> 00:18:00.440
one consistent pattern. Every master is

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:02.720
a student for life. And you can be a

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:04.720
genuine student [music] if you're hiding

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:07.440
behind a mask of mastery. You know, the

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:09.240
biggest benefit of intelligence is not

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.400
the end of ignorance, it's the end of

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.480
pretending.

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:15.360
You know,

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:19.000
we're surrounded by endless images of

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:21.960
flawless people in their flawless poses,

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:25.200
flawlessly photoshopped. But,

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:28.480
in the end, all art is about asymmetry.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:30.600
We're beautiful because we're broken.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:33.520
Because the real purpose of

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:36.320
intelligence, of this thing called life,

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:37.442
is to travel far and wide only to return

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:37.840
to yourself

00:18:37.442 --> 00:18:41.159
>> [music]

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:44.040
>> and fully accept who you are.

00:18:41.159 --> 00:18:45.302
That is your truest intelligence. If you

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:45.400
like this video,

00:18:45.302 --> 00:18:47.159
>> [music]

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:49.320
>> don't forget to subscribe. And if you

00:18:47.159 --> 00:18:52.000
want to use AI to start a business,

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:54.679
here's another video where I walk you

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:55.760
through exactly what I would do.

00:18:54.679 --> 00:18:58.440
Thank you,

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:58.440
and I love you.

